  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 237 AM CDT Wed Sep 23 2026\n\nPatchy fog will be possible across the region again this morning. \nWarm air advection in the 700mb layer will spark off some high based \nshowers over Nebraska early this morning. Some of this lift could \nextend further south for some high based rain showers to develop \nover parts of central Kansas this morning.  \n\nMeanwhile current satellite water vapor shows upper troughing over \nthe southwestern states with embedded short waves inducing a plume \nof richer deep moisture to stream northward across New Mexico/Texas \npanhandle. This axis of rich moisture will shift slightly eastward \nwith numerous showers/storms expected to develop over eastern \nColorado/western Kansas today through Thursday. Some of this \nactivity will gradually spread east into central Kansas on Thursday. \nThe better chances for showers/storms looks to occur Thursday night-\nFriday mainly affecting central/south central Kansas. Limited \ninstability and poor mid-level lapse rates will keep severe weather \npotential low this period. Models remain consistent with a final \nupper level short wave moving across Kansas on Saturday, but show \nsome timing differences with this upper level wave. The Saturday \nperiod continues to show a slight uptick in instability which could \nspark off a few strong storms Saturday afternoon/evening. Timing of \nthe upper level wave moving across Kansas with this possible uptick \nin instability will be the key thing to watch.\n\nSunday looks to be dry in the wake of Saturday's system moving east \nof Kansas. Longer range models/GEFS ensemble mean continue to \nforecast a more pronounced upper level wave moving across the \nRockies and ejecting into Kansas for Monday night into Tuesday. This \nperiod has potential for ingredients coming together to produce \nstrong/severe storms. We will continue to watch model trends for \nthis period since it's still several days away.",
